# Notification Banner & Toast

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A notice, an outcome and a short message are three different things. A banner carries a notice that applies to the whole page or the outcome of the previous step. A toast is a brief message at the bottom of the screen. The notification drawer keeps persistent notifications until someone dismisses them.

Home Assistant provides the toast and the drawer. The banner is a Civica pattern. For persistent notification actions, see [Notifications, acknowledgement and alerts](../home-assistant/notifications-and-delivery.html). For the unread count on the sidebar, see [Badge](notification-badge.html). The nearest native message box is the alert, on [Alert & Tip Callout](alert-and-tip-callout.html).

This page covers:

- Notification Banner & Toast
  - `ha-toast`
  - `notification-drawer`
  - Civica Notification Banner Pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- A toast is one line of text, an optional action button and an optional close button, on a bar that is dark in light mode and light in dark mode.
- The drawer is a panel that slides in from the side, with a title, a close button, a list of notifications with the newest first, and a “Dismiss all” button.
- A banner is a bordered block with a title bar, a short heading and a sentence of detail.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-ha-toast"><code>ha-toast</code></h3>

A short message that closes by itself. Home Assistant shows one for things like a saved setting or a copied link. Dashboard configuration cannot create one, and `card-mod` does not reach it, because it sits outside every card.

- **Timing.** It closes after 4 seconds by default. A shorter time is raised to 4 seconds. A toast can carry a close button and one or two action buttons.
- **Announcement.** The text is also placed in a hidden polite live region, so assistive technology can announce it without moving focus. The announcement can differ from the visible text.
- **Size.** It is at least 48 pixels tall and up to 650 pixels wide. Below 450 pixels wide or 500 pixels tall it fills the width of the screen with square corners.
- **Stacking.** Several toasts stack.

The text colour is `ha-color-on-neutral-loud` and the background is `ha-color-neutral-10`. The Civica theme sets the text colour to `#FFFFFF` in light mode and `#0B0C0C` in dark mode. It leaves the background at Home Assistant’s `#202020` in light mode and sets it to `#CCCCCC` in dark mode, so the bar is light with dark text. Calculated from those colours, the text and background make 16.29 to 1 in light mode and 12.20 to 1 in dark mode, in every Civica theme.

A toast gives people about four seconds to read it, so it is easy to miss. Use a toast only for a result that is also visible somewhere else. Put anything people must act on in a persistent notification.

<h3 id="child-notification-drawer"><code>notification-drawer</code></h3>

The element’s class is `HuiNotificationDrawer`.

It is a modal side panel that Home Assistant opens when someone asks for their notifications. It is not a card and cannot be set up in YAML.

- It lists persistent notifications and configuration prompts, newest first.
- “Dismiss all” appears only when there is more than one persistent notification.
- With no notifications it shows an empty message.
- Escape closes it, and so does a change of page. The close button is labelled.
- It is up to 500 pixels wide, and the full width of a narrow screen.

It uses the theme’s page background (`#FFFFFF` in light mode, `#17191B` in dark mode), primary text colour (`#0B0C0C`, `#FFFFFF`) and divider colour (`#CECECE`, `#50565A`). `card-mod` does not reach it.

<h3 id="child-civica-notification-banner-pattern"><code>Civica Notification Banner Pattern</code></h3>

A banner carries a notice that applies to the whole page, or the outcome of the previous step. Home Assistant has no banner element and the theme adds none; the banner is a Civica pattern for guide pages and your own markup. Persistent notifications go to the drawer and toasts vanish, so neither replaces it.

The theme provides the colour pairs, which are the same in light and dark modes:

| Kind | Fill | Text on it |
|---|---|---|
| Notice | `civica-notice-fill` `#0B0C0C` | `civica-on-notice` `#FFFFFF` |
| Success | `civica-success-fill` `#0F7A52` | `civica-on-success` `#FFFFFF` |
| Danger | `civica-danger-fill` `#CA3535` | `civica-on-danger` `#FFFFFF` |
| Warning | `civica-warning-fill` `#FFDD00` | `civica-on-warning` `#0B0C0C` |

Use a fill with its matching text colour, not white on `error-color`, which changes in dark mode. Calculated from the colours, white on the success fill is 5.35 to 1 and white on the danger fill is 5.16 to 1.

Rules for the banner:

- Carry a service-wide notice or an outcome, not field validation. Keep an error with its field, and do not let a success compete with an error summary.
- Show one coherent notice at a time.
- Say what is affected and when. A temporary integration outage is not evidence that a device is off.
- Word success from the result Home Assistant has confirmed, not because a button was pressed.
- Remove a stale success notice after navigation.

For how long a service-wide banner stays, see [Global banner](../publishing/components/global-banner.html). For urgent messages, see [Emergency banner](../publishing/components/emergency-banner.html). A maintenance notice is not urgent just because it is a notice.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A toast confirms a result without taking over the screen.
- A toast closes by itself, so people can miss it.
- The toast text colour and its background both change with the mode.
- The drawer keeps notifications until they are dismissed.
- Neither the toast nor the drawer can be created or restyled from a dashboard.
- Home Assistant has no banner element, so a banner is your own markup.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Give a banner on a page a labelled region and a meaningful heading. For a result that appears after the page has loaded, use one announcement and do not repeat it for every refresh. Do not move focus for routine background updates. Do not use `role="alert"` for routine notices.

Use the same wording for the same event. Never use a toast as the only record of an error.

Test notices with errors, narrow screens, long translations and updates that arrive while someone is typing. Check them in light and dark modes, with two theme colours, at 320 CSS pixels, using [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: a banner is a region with a heading.
- 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum) and 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: check text on the toast and banner fills in each mode.
- 2.2.1 Timing Adjustable: a toast with an action closes after 4 seconds by default.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: a toast has a polite live announcement, and a static banner has none.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard and 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap: the drawer is modal and closes with Escape.
- 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum): a toast at the bottom can cover content.
- 3.3.1 Error Identification: errors stay with the field, not a banner.

